# Project: UX Research Interview Assistant + Affinity Mapper
# A web app that helps UX researchers generate interview questions, run sessions, and build an affinity map from AI-generated or manually added themes.
# Designed in Figma (mid-fidelity wireframes) first, then built in Cursor.

## Project Overview
Three-phase tool for UX researchers:
1. **Setup** — enter a research topic and starter questions; Claude generates motivation-focused follow-up questions (WHY-oriented, not surface-level)
2. **Interview** — display generated questions during the session alongside a typed notes panel and a session timer
3. **Affinity Board** — AI clusters input (uploaded audio transcript via Whisper OR pasted raw notes) into themes displayed as sticky notes; researcher can manually add, edit, and delete themes

## Design Process
- UI is designed in Figma as mid-fidelity wireframes before any code is written
- Figma wireframes are the source of truth for layout, spacing, and interaction model
- Build in Cursor follows the Figma spec — do not invent layout decisions in code
- Three screens are wireframed: Setup, Interview, Affinity Board
- Affinity Board is the primary screen — it gets the most design attention

## Stack
- **Frontend**: React (Vite)
- **Styling**: Tailwind CSS
- **Design**: Figma (mid-fidelity wireframes)
- **AI**: Anthropic Claude API (claude-sonnet-4-20250514) for question generation and theme clustering
- **Transcription**: OpenAI Whisper API for audio file upload → transcript
- **Drag and drop**: @dnd-kit/core (stretch goal only)
- **State**: React useState / useReducer; no external state library unless complexity demands it
- **Storage**: localStorage for session persistence; no backend required

## File Structure
```
src/
  components/
    QuestionPanel.jsx         # displays AI-generated questions during interview
    NotesPanel.jsx            # typed notes alongside questions
    SessionTimer.jsx          # elapsed timer with start/pause/reset
    AffinityBoard.jsx         # sticky-note grid with add, edit, delete
    StickyNote.jsx            # individual theme card with label + quotes
    ui/                       # shared atoms: Button, Card, Tag, Badge
  lib/
    claude.js                 # all Anthropic API calls
    prompts.js                # prompt templates
    whisper.js                # audio file upload + transcription logic
  hooks/
    useSession.js             # session state across all three phases
    useAffinity.js            # theme CRUD and board state
    useTimer.js               # session timer logic
  pages/
    Setup.jsx
    Interview.jsx
    AffinityBoard.jsx
  App.jsx
  main.jsx
```

## AI Integration Rules
- All Anthropic API calls go through `lib/claude.js`
- All Whisper calls go through `lib/whisper.js`
- Never expose API keys in client code — use environment variables:
  - `VITE_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`
  - `VITE_OPENAI_API_KEY`
- Always handle loading, error, and empty states for every AI call
- Prompt templates live in `lib/prompts.js` — keep them readable and iterable

## Prompting Guidelines
- **Question generation prompt**: take topic + starter questions → return array of 6–8 follow-up questions focused on uncovering participant motivations and "why" behind behaviors; avoid surface-level or yes/no questions
- **Theme clustering prompt**: take transcript or raw notes → return array of theme objects: { id, label, quotes: [], color }; themes should reflect behavioral patterns and motivations, not just topics
- Both prompts must return structured JSON so UI can render predictably

## Affinity Board Rules
- Initial themes are AI-generated but fully editable
- Researcher can: add a new theme (blank sticky), edit theme label inline, delete a theme
- Each sticky note shows: theme label + 1–2 supporting quotes from the transcript
- Color coding: AI assigns a color per theme from a fixed pastel palette
- Board layout: CSS grid for MVP — drag and drop is a stretch goal only
- Input toggle at top: "Upload Audio" vs "Paste Notes" — both feed into the same Claude clustering prompt

## Interview View Rules
- Left panel: AI-generated questions (read-only, scrollable)
- Right panel: free-form typed notes (textarea, auto-saves to localStorage)
- Top bar: session timer (elapsed time, start/pause/reset)
- No API calls during Interview view — purely local

## UI / UX Principles
- Match the Figma wireframes exactly for layout and information hierarchy
- Setup and Interview: minimal, distraction-free, calm
- Affinity Board: warmer, more visual — sticky note aesthetic with pastel colors
- Desktop-first; mobile-friendly is a stretch goal

## Do Not
- Do not invent layout decisions in code — follow the Figma wireframes
- Do not add live in-browser audio recording — audio is uploaded as a file after the session
- Do not add user auth or a database
- Do not add cross-participant comparison in MVP
- Do not use Redux or Zustand
- Do not hardcode any API keys
- Do not make the affinity board drag-and-drop in MVP

## Constraints & Assumptions
- One researcher, one session at a time
- Sessions persist in localStorage between page refreshes
- The tool assists synthesis; the researcher has final control over all themes
- Affinity board is the core deliverable — Setup and Interview views support it
